---
// src/components/HobbyIslandSection.astro
//
// New section in the `.sky-chain` run, sitting between
// AchievementIslandSection and BonusIslandSection (update the import +
// insertion in index.astro — see the usage example at the bottom of
// this comment).
//
// v2 — reworked after seeing your layered mockup. The three artworks
// (`backIslandImage`, `joyconImage`, `frontIslandImage`) are NOT three
// independent, independently-positioned islands like
// AchievementIslandSection's statue/temple — they're one single
// island illustration split across three files that only line up
// correctly when stacked directly on top of each other, back to
// front, with no relative offset between them.
//
// I confirmed this by feature-matching each of your three original
// uploads against your mockup image (AKAZE + RANSAC homography): all
// three map onto it with the SAME scale (~0.446x) and effectively
// zero relative translation — i.e. your mockup is just "take each
// full original canvas, scale it down by the same amount, lay them
// directly on top of each other." So rather than eyeballing three
// separate `top`/`left` positions (which is what v1 of this file did,
// and why the pieces looked scattered instead of interlocked), the
// three delivered images this time are all cropped from the exact
// SAME rectangle of their original canvases and share IDENTICAL
// pixel dimensions (1900x1028) — stack them with `inset: 0` and they
// land in register automatically, the same way your mockup does:
//
//   1. `backIslandImage`  (bed, first-aid kit, board game, campfire) — bottom layer
//   2. `joyconImage`      (the pink Joy-Con)                          — middle layer
//   3. `frontIslandImage` (fountain, pool, piano, palm tree, ...)     — top layer
//
// IMPORTANT for future edits: if any of these three files ever gets
// re-cropped or re-exported, it has to be cropped from that same
// shared rectangle (or just re-derived from the original uncropped
// canvas at the same scale) — cropping one independently of the
// other two will shift it relative to the others and the island will
// visibly come apart at the seams. There's no per-image `top`/`left`
// tuning in the CSS below to compensate with; alignment is baked into
// the image files themselves, not the layout.
//
// v4 — per your request, the three layers now drift INDEPENDENTLY on
// cursor proximity instead of moving as one rigid unit. Worth calling
// out plainly: this trades away the pixel-perfect interlock from v2/
// v3. At rest (cursor far away, or `prefers-reduced-motion`) all
// three still line up exactly, since every layer's drift settles back
// to `translate(0, 0)` — the seams only start to show once the cursor
// is close enough to actually push them apart. Kept the push small
// (see `data-depth` below) specifically to keep that gap subtle
// rather than letting the artwork visibly tear; turn the depth
// numbers down further (or back to identical) if it reads as too much
// once you see it live.
//
// Each layer gets its own two-level wrapper, same reasoning as v3 for
// why bob and drift can't share an element (a running CSS animation
// silently wins over a JS-set inline `transform` on the same
// property): a `.hobby-layer-bob` wrapper (own bob timing) around a
// `.hobby-layer-drift.parallax-inner` wrapper (own mouse-repel
// strength via `data-depth`). `data-depth` is read by the shared
// script below as a multiplier on the base push distance — back
// island 0.55x (subtle, reads as "further away"), Joy-Con 0.85x,
// front island 1.2x (strongest, reads as "closest to the viewer") —
// a small depth-of-field cue on top of the independence you asked
// for, rather than three identical pushes that just happen to be
// computed separately. Bob timing is also offset per layer (different
// duration + a negative `animation-delay` to start mid-cycle) so they
// don't bob in lockstep either. `joyconImage` is still optional (omit
// it and the other two layers still render/drift fine on their own).
//
// v5 — per your request the island is now A LOT bigger. The previous
// approach (v3/v4) kept the island absolutely positioned, bottom-
// anchored inside a fixed `100vh` section, with `.content` pinned to
// the top — which meant the island's size was always fighting the
// text for a slice of one fixed-height box, and capping it by
// viewport height (`min(..., 86vh)`) to avoid that fight quietly
// capped it fairly small on ordinary monitors too (that's why the
// previous bump barely read as bigger). This version removes that
// fight instead of continuing to referee it: the island is back in
// NORMAL DOCUMENT FLOW, stacked below `.content` (both centered via
// `display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center` on
// `.hobby-section`) rather than absolutely positioned over it. With
// `min-height: 100vh` (not a fixed `height`), the section is simply
// as tall as it needs to be to fit both comfortably — on most screens
// that's now taller than one viewport, so the very bottom of the
// island sits slightly below the fold, which is the expected trade
// for "a lot bigger" rather than a bug. No vh-based caps or
// short-viewport media queries are needed anymore — normal flow can't
// overlap by construction, at any viewport size, so `width` is just a
// plain `clamp()` now.
//
// Background: `hobby_island_background.webp` is a FLAT fill (sampled
// at ≈#01d4f3), close enough to identical to `--dusk-blue` (defined
// in Layout.astro; AchievementIslandSection's bottom edge and
// BonusIslandSection's top edge both already use it) that this
// section's seam-fades reuse `--dusk-blue` on BOTH edges. That means
// NEITHER Layout.astro NOR AchievementIslandSection.astro NOR
// BonusIslandSection.astro need any changes — all three already
// agree on this exact color at the seam.
//
// v6 — you swapped in your own re-exported CSP layers and the layout
// broke ("look size and position of the site completely changes").
// I checked the three files you sent: back island came in at
// 2002x1035, front island at 3192x1942, Joy-Con at 513x931 — three
// completely different aspect ratios, each unrelated to the other two
// and to the 1900x1028 shared canvas this component was built around.
//
// Root cause: CSP's "export selected layer" trims each layer to its
// own visible-content bounding box before saving. That's exactly what
// v2's top comment warned about — cropping one layer independently of
// the others destroys the relative positioning between them — except
// now it's happening on every export instead of once. Once a layer is
// trimmed, the empty space that encoded "this layer sits 400px lower
// and 200px right of that layer" is just gone from the file. There's
// no metadata recording the trim offset either (checked — none of the
// three files carry it), so there's no way for CODE to reconstruct
// where the layers should sit relative to each other after the fact.
// This genuinely can't be fixed by changing CSS/JS; it has to be fixed
// at export time, by not trimming in the first place.
//
// THE FIX (do this in Clip Studio Paint, takes effect on every future
// export, no code changes ever needed again):
//   Don't use "Export Selected Layer" — that's the command that trims.
//   Instead, for each of the three layers: hide every OTHER layer
//   (toggle their eye icons off) so only the one you want is visible,
//   then use the regular whole-image export you'd use for finished art
//   (File > Export > PNG/JPEG/... — NOT the per-layer export command).
//   That command always exports the full canvas size, transparent
//   wherever nothing's drawn — so if your canvas is 3840x2160, all
//   three exports come out 3840x2160 every time, in the same
//   coordinate system, no matter how much you keep editing the art
//   inside them. Repeat for the other two layers. Drop the three
//   resulting files in as backIslandImage/joyconImage/frontIslandImage
//   — same filenames is fine, no code change needed on your end.
//
// To make THIS FILE robust to that workflow going forward (so if your
// canvas size ever changes — e.g. you resize the CSP document — it's
// a two-number prop edit instead of another round-trip with me), the
// hardcoded `aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2079` is now driven by two new
// OPTIONAL props, `canvasWidth` / `canvasHeight`, defaulting to
// 3840 / 2160 (a plain, untrimmed canvas — no "-79" fudge, since that
// number only existed to match the old, already-cropped 1900x1028
// deliverables). If your actual CSP canvas is a different size, pass
// it in; otherwise leave the props off and the defaults apply.
//
// I can't repair the three files you just sent — the alignment info
// they'd need is already gone from the trimmed images themselves, not
// just missing from the code — so please re-export following the
// steps above and drop the new files in; everything else about this
// component (stacking, independent drift, bob, sizing) stays exactly
// as it was.
//
// v7 — added an optional `backIslandFrozenImage` layer that overlays
// the back island and periodically "reveals" itself (spreads out from
// its own center, holds for a few seconds, then withdraws back to the
// center the same way) at random intervals, fully hands-off once it's
// on the page. SUPERSEDED BY v12 BELOW: the random-timer trigger
// described in point 3 is gone — it's a hover reveal now. Points 1 and
// 2 (alignment, the circle-reveal mechanic) still apply as-is; left
// this section intact rather than rewriting it, same as how v2/v3
// stay in place after later versions changed that behavior too. Three
// implementation notes (again, point 3 is historical):
//
//   1. Alignment: this overlay lives INSIDE the back island's own
//      `.hobby-layer-drift` wrapper (not a sibling slot like Joy-Con/
//      front island), stacked directly on top of `backIslandImage`
//      with `position: absolute; inset: 0`. That means it moves and
//      bobs together with the back island as one piece (correct — it's
//      frost ON the island, not a separate floating object), and it
//      only lines up correctly if it's exported at the exact same
//      canvas size as the rest (see v6) — confirmed your upload is
//      3840x2160, matching.
//
//   2. The "spreads out from the center, holds, withdraws" motion is a
//      `clip-path: circle()` reveal, not a scale/opacity transform —
//      animating the circle's radius from 0% to comfortably past the
//      image's farthest corner (150%, overshooting on purpose so the
//      exact aspect ratio doesn't matter) and back reads as the frost
//      actually spreading across the existing artwork, rather than the
//      whole overlay image growing/fading in place.
//
//   3. "From time to time (randomly)" is handled entirely in the
//      script below with `setTimeout` + `Math.random()` — no fixed
//      loop/interval, so appearances don't fall into a predictable
//      rhythm. Each cycle: wait a random 8-22s, play the ~6.5s reveal-
//      hold-withdraw animation once, then pick a new random wait and
//      repeat, forever, starting from when the page loads. Skipped
//      entirely under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, consistent
//      with how the bob/drift are already handled elsewhere in this
//      file. Omit the prop and nothing changes — no overlay element is
//      even rendered.
//
// v8 — added a hover swap on the palm tree: `palmTreeImage` (pulled
// out of `frontIslandImage` into its own layer) is the default, and
// hovering it cross-fades in `palmTree80sImage` with a top-to-bottom
// wipe, reversing the same way on mouse-out. A few things worth
// calling out:
//
//   - Both new images are OPTIONAL and layer inside the FRONT island's
//     existing `.hobby-layer-drift` (same reasoning as the v7 frozen
//     overlay on the back island: it needs to bob/drift together with
//     the rest of that island, not move independently), stacked in
//     this order: `frontIslandImage`, then `palmTreeImage`, then the
//     hover hotspot, then `palmTree80sImage`. Both tree images must be
//     exported at the same canvas size as everything else (see v6) —
//     confirmed your two uploads are both 3840x2160.
//
//   - The reveal itself is a plain CSS `clip-path: inset()` transition
//     driven by `:hover` — no JS state machine needed. At rest,
//     `palmTree80sImage` is clipped away entirely except a 0px sliver
//     at its own top edge (`inset(0 0 100% 0)`); hovering animates
//     that to `inset(0 0 0% 0)`, i.e. the clipped-away region shrinks
//     from the bottom up, which reads as the 80s art wiping DOWN over
//     the normal tree. Un-hovering just transitions back, so the wipe
//     reverses on its own — no separate "hide" animation to maintain.
//
//   - The trickier part: `.hobby-island-group` has `pointer-events:
//     none` on it (so none of this artwork ever intercepts clicks on
//     the page, and so the mouse-repel drift's own `mousemove`
//     listener — which reads plain viewport coordinates, not real
//     hover state — isn't affected). Naively adding `:hover` to one of
//     the tree images directly wouldn't fire at all because of that.
//     Also, using the tree's full layer box (the whole `inset: 0` slot
//     — mostly transparent canvas padding) as the hover target would
//     trigger from anywhere near the front island, not just "when you
//     moved over" the tree itself. Both problems are solved by one
//     small invisible `.hobby-palm-hotspot` div, explicitly opted back
//     into `pointer-events: auto` (an element CAN re-enable pointer
//     events even under an ancestor's `pointer-events: none` — it's
//     not inherited as a hard block, just a default), sized to the
//     tree's actual painted pixels rather than its canvas-sized
//     bounding box.
//
//   - That sizing is computed at runtime, not hardcoded: the script
//     below draws `palmTreeImage` to an offscreen canvas once it's
//     loaded, scans its alpha channel for the real ink bounding box,
//     and positions the hotspot from that (as percentages, so it still
//     tracks correctly at any rendered size). Given how often you're
//     re-exporting these layers, hand-picked percentages would've gone
//     stale the next time the tree moves or resizes within the canvas
//     — this way there's nothing to keep in sync by hand, ever, same
//     spirit as v6/v7. Needs `palmTreeImage` to be same-origin (true
//     for your site's own `/images/` assets) since cross-origin canvas
//     pixel reads throw; that's caught, and the hotspot just falls
//     back to covering the whole slot rather than breaking anything.
//
// v9 — v8's reveal was a pure overlay: `palmTree80sImage` wiped in on
// TOP of `palmTreeImage`, which stayed fully opaque underneath the
// whole time. Since the 80s art isn't the same silhouette as the
// normal tree (different fronds, no Kirby, wider canopy), the two
// don't mask each other pixel-for-pixel, so bits of the normal tree
// showed through wherever the 80s layer above it was transparent —
// exactly what you flagged. Fixed by making `.hobby-palm-base` clip
// itself away in lockstep with `.hobby-palm-80s` revealing, using the
// same `inset()` property, direction, duration, and easing on both —
// so the region the 80s tree has revealed so far is ALWAYS exactly
// the region the normal tree has just clipped away, with the boundary
// itself moving as one edge instead of two independent wipes that
// happen to overlap. At any instant exactly one image is visible at
// each point on the tree; never both, never neither. Also added a
// thin synthwave-gradient "scanline" that sweeps down with it (a
// one-shot `animation`, not a `transition`, since it needs to fade in
// AND back out rather than settle at an end state) — the glowing
// leading edge is what pushes this from "wipe" to "80s computery
// digitizing effect," which is what you asked for instead of a plain
// cross-fade/reveal.
//
// v10 — two fixes to that scanline, per your feedback (full reasoning
// is inline as a comment right above `.hobby-palm-scanline` below):
// it was spanning the whole front island instead of just the tree
// (moved it from a sibling of the hotspot to a CHILD of it, so it
// inherits the hotspot's already-correct tree-sized box for free), and
// it's now a thinner cyan/magenta hairline pair with a stepped
// (not smooth) sweep for a more "electronic" feel, per your request.
//
// v11 — pushed "more electronic" further with two additions, both
// full reasoning inline near the relevant rules below: a brightness/
// hue flicker on `palmTree80sImage` itself as it locks in (a signal-
// settling stutter, only on the way in — the normal tree still just
// clips cleanly away), and an irregular horizontal jitter on the scan
// line as it sweeps down (signal noise, not a deliberate wobble). Both
// ride on top of the existing v9/v10 mechanics rather than replacing
// anything — the clip-path sync that guarantees no double-exposure
// and the tree-scoped hotspot positioning are untouched.
//
// v12 — the back island frost (v7) used to appear on a random timer;
// now it's a hover reveal instead, matching the palm tree pattern
// (v8): a `.hobby-frost-hotspot` div, alpha-scanned to the frost art's
// own painted pixels (not the whole back island), and
// `.hobby-frozen-overlay` transitions its `clip-path` on `:hover`
// instead of running a fixed 6.5s keyframe loop — it grows while
// you're over the hotspot and withdraws the moment you leave, for as
// long as the hover lasts, rather than a timed appear-hold-withdraw
// cycle running on its own. The random-interval `setTimeout` scheduler
// from v7 is gone entirely. Along the way, refactored the alpha-scan
// logic (used to size the palm tree's hotspot) into a shared
// `fitHotspotToAlpha()` helper in the script below, since it's now
// doing the same job twice — same behavior as before for the palm
// tree, just de-duplicated rather than copy-pasted a second time for
// the frost hotspot.
//
// v13 — slowed the frost hover transition from 0.6s to 1.1s, per your
// feedback. Only the frost — the palm tree hover swap stays at 0.6s.
//
// v14 — added the Monado sword: `monadoImage` is a plain new layer,
// LAST in the front island's stack so it renders in front of
// everything else there (per "part of the front island in the front
// of it"). `monadoSymbolImages` is an array of interchangeable symbol
// images for the sword's gem — I checked your five uploads and they
// all share the identical painted bounding box, so this needed no
// hotspot/alignment work at all (unlike palm tree/frost): just one
// `.is-active` symbol shown at a time, swapping to a different random
// one at a random 3-7s interval, forever, starting immediately on
// load so the gem is never empty. Full reasoning for the flicker-in
// swap (and why the outgoing symbol gets a hard cut instead of a
// fade, to avoid the same double-exposure problem v9 solved for the
// palm tree) is inline near `.hobby-monado-symbol` below.
//
// Carries `.sky-chain` (collapses the gap on both sides) and
// `.halftone-hover` (opts into the single page-wide cursor-spotlight
// element from Layout.astro) — same as every other section in the
// chain, no extra markup/script needed for either.
//
// Usage — insert in src/pages/index.astro between
// AchievementIslandSection and BonusIslandSection:
//
//   import HobbyIslandSection from '../components/HobbyIslandSection.astro';
//
//   <HobbyIslandSection
//     id="section-hobbies"
//     eyebrow="Island 05"
//     title="Ut Labore Et Dolore"
//     text="Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque laudantium, totam rem aperiam."
//     backIslandImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_back_island.webp"
//     joyconImage="/personal-cv-page/images/joycon.webp"
//     frontIslandImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_front_island.webp"
//     backgroundImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_island_background.webp"
//     backIslandFrozenImage="/personal-cv-page/images/hobby_back_island_frozen.webp"
//     palmTreeImage="/personal-cv-page/images/palm_tree.webp"
//     palmTree80sImage="/personal-cv-page/images/palm_tree_80s.webp"
//     monadoImage="/personal-cv-page/images/monado.webp"
//     monadoSymbolImages={[
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_1.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_2.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_3.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_4.webp",
//       "/personal-cv-page/images/monado_symbol_5.webp",
//     ]}
//     canvasWidth={3840}
//     canvasHeight={2160}
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  backIslandImage: string;
  joyconImage?: string;
  frontIslandImage: string;
  backgroundImage: string;
  /** Optional overlay that periodically reveals itself on top of the
   *  back island (see the v7 comment above). Must be exported at the
   *  same canvas size as the other layers to line up. Omit it and no
   *  overlay element is rendered at all. */
  backIslandFrozenImage?: string;
  /** Optional palm tree layer, pulled out of `frontIslandImage` (see
   *  the v8 comment above). Renders as a normal part of the front
   *  island if `palmTree80sImage` is omitted; pass both to enable the
   *  hover swap. Same canvas-size requirement as every other layer. */
  palmTreeImage?: string;
  /** Optional hover-alternate for `palmTreeImage` — wipes in top-to-
   *  bottom on hover, reverses on mouse-out. Ignored if `palmTreeImage`
   *  isn't also provided. */
  palmTree80sImage?: string;
  /** Optional Monado sword layer, sitting in FRONT of the rest of the
   *  front island (see the v14 comment above). Same canvas-size
   *  requirement as every other layer. */
  monadoImage?: string;
  /** Optional array of symbol images for the sword's gem — one is
   *  shown at a time, swapping to a random different one at random
   *  intervals for as long as the page is open. All must share the
   *  exact same painted position (yours do — same bounding box on all
   *  five). Ignored if `monadoImage` isn't also provided. */
  monadoSymbolImages?: string[];
  /** Your CSP document's canvas size in px — every layer export must
   *  share this exact size (see the v6 comment above). Defaults to
   *  3840x2160; override only if your actual CSP canvas differs. */
  canvasWidth?: number;
  canvasHeight?: number;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  backIslandImage,
  joyconImage,
  frontIslandImage,
  backgroundImage,
  backIslandFrozenImage,
  palmTreeImage,
  palmTree80sImage,
  monadoImage,
  monadoSymbolImages,
  canvasWidth = 3840,
  canvasHeight = 2160,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section
  class="hobby-section sky-chain seam-fade-top seam-fade-bottom halftone-hover"
  id={id}
  style={`background-image: url(${backgroundImage});`}
>
  <div class="content">
    {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
    <h2>{title}</h2>
    <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="hobby-island-group" aria-hidden="true">
    <div
      class="hobby-island-group-inner"
      style={`aspect-ratio: ${canvasWidth} / ${canvasHeight};`}
    >
      <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-back">
        <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-back">
          <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="0.55">
            <img class="hobby-layer" src={backIslandImage} alt="" />
            {backIslandFrozenImage && (
              <>
                <div class="hobby-frost-hotspot" data-frost-hotspot></div>
                <img
                  class="hobby-layer hobby-frozen-overlay"
                  src={backIslandFrozenImage}
                  alt=""
                  data-frozen-overlay
                />
              </>
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {joyconImage && (
        <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-joycon">
          <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-joycon">
            <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="0.85">
              <img class="hobby-layer" src={joyconImage} alt="" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      <div class="hobby-layer-slot hobby-layer-slot-front">
        <div class="hobby-layer-bob hobby-layer-bob-front">
          <div class="hobby-layer-drift parallax-inner" data-depth="1.2">
            <img class="hobby-layer" src={frontIslandImage} alt="" />
            {palmTreeImage && palmTree80sImage && (
              <div class="hobby-palm-hotspot" data-palm-hotspot>
                <div class="hobby-palm-scanline" aria-hidden="true"></div>
              </div>
            )}
            {palmTreeImage && (
              <img
                class="hobby-layer hobby-palm-base"
                src={palmTreeImage}
                alt=""
                data-palm-base
              />
            )}
            {palmTreeImage && palmTree80sImage && (
              <img
                class="hobby-layer hobby-palm-80s"
                src={palmTree80sImage}
                alt=""
              />
            )}
            {monadoImage && (
              <img class="hobby-layer hobby-monado" src={monadoImage} alt="" />
            )}
            {monadoImage &&
              monadoSymbolImages &&
              monadoSymbolImages.map((src) => (
                <img
                  class="hobby-layer hobby-monado-symbol"
                  src={src}
                  alt=""
                  data-monado-symbol
                />
              ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  // Same proximity-free mouse-repel drift as the rest of this family
  // (see AchievementIslandSection.astro / ComputerIslandSection.astro
  // for the original) — every `.parallax-inner` element on the page
  // eases toward a small offset away from the cursor, falling off
  // with distance. Copied verbatim rather than shared, matching how
  // every other section in this family ships its own copy. Extended
  // with one thing the original didn't need: an optional `data-depth`
  // attribute (see the markup above) that scales `maxOffset` per
  // element, so the three layers here each get a different push
  // strength instead of all three computing the exact same offset —
  // reading `data-depth` and defaulting to `1` when it's absent means
  // this still behaves identically to the original for every plain
  // `.parallax-inner` element elsewhere on the page.
  const islands = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".parallax-inner");
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia(
    "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)"
  ).matches;

  if (islands.length && !prefersReducedMotion) {
    const maxOffset = 36; // px, strongest push when cursor is right on top of the island
    const influenceRadius = 500; // px, push fades to 0 beyond this distance
    const ease = 0.08;

    let mouseX = 0;
    let mouseY = 0;
    let hasMouse = false;

    const state = Array.from(islands).map((el) => ({
      el,
      depth: parseFloat(el.dataset.depth ?? "1"),
      currentX: 0,
      currentY: 0,
    }));

    window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
      mouseX = e.clientX;
      mouseY = e.clientY;
      hasMouse = true;
    });

    function tick() {
      for (const s of state) {
        let targetX = 0;
        let targetY = 0;

        if (hasMouse) {
          const rect = s.el.getBoundingClientRect();
          const restX = rect.left + rect.width / 2 - s.currentX;
          const restY = rect.top + rect.height / 2 - s.currentY;

          const dx = restX - mouseX;
          const dy = restY - mouseY;
          const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1;

          if (dist < influenceRadius) {
            const falloff = 1 - dist / influenceRadius;
            const nx = dx / dist;
            const ny = dy / dist;
            targetX = nx * maxOffset * s.depth * falloff;
            targetY = ny * maxOffset * s.depth * falloff;
          }
        }

        s.currentX += (targetX - s.currentX) * ease;
        s.currentY += (targetY - s.currentY) * ease;
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(${s.currentX.toFixed(2)}px, ${s.currentY.toFixed(2)}px)`;
      }
      requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  // Shared by both hover hotspots below (palm tree, frost — v8 and
  // v12 respectively): sizes `hotspotEl` (an invisible
  // `pointer-events: auto` div) to the ACTUAL painted pixels of
  // `sourceImg`, not its full (mostly transparent) canvas-sized layer
  // box — otherwise hovering anywhere on that island would trigger the
  // effect, not just the specific artwork it belongs to. Computed once
  // from the image's alpha channel via an offscreen canvas rather than
  // hardcoded, so re-exporting either layer at a different position/
  // size later doesn't require touching this file again. Needs
  // `sourceImg` to be same-origin (true for your site's own `/images/`
  // assets) since cross-origin canvas pixel reads throw; that's
  // caught, and the hotspot just falls back to covering the whole slot
  // (its CSS default) rather than breaking anything.
  function fitHotspotToAlpha(
    sourceImg: HTMLImageElement,
    hotspotEl: HTMLElement
  ) {
    const run = () => {
      try {
        const w = sourceImg.naturalWidth;
        const h = sourceImg.naturalHeight;
        if (!w || !h) return;

        const canvas = document.createElement("canvas");
        canvas.width = w;
        canvas.height = h;
        const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
        if (!ctx) return;

        ctx.drawImage(sourceImg, 0, 0);
        const { data } = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, w, h);

        let minX = w;
        let minY = h;
        let maxX = 0;
        let maxY = 0;
        let found = false;
        const step = 2; // sample every other pixel on both axes — 4x fewer reads, plenty precise for a hover zone

        for (let y = 0; y < h; y += step) {
          for (let x = 0; x < w; x += step) {
            const alpha = data[(y * w + x) * 4 + 3];
            if (alpha > 10) {
              found = true;
              if (x < minX) minX = x;
              if (x > maxX) maxX = x;
              if (y < minY) minY = y;
              if (y > maxY) maxY = y;
            }
          }
        }

        if (!found) return;

        // Small padding so the hotspot isn't pixel-tight against the
        // artwork's edge — makes it a little more forgiving to hover.
        const padX = (maxX - minX) * 0.05;
        const padY = (maxY - minY) * 0.05;
        const left = Math.max(0, minX - padX);
        const top = Math.max(0, minY - padY);
        const right = Math.min(w, maxX + padX);
        const bottom = Math.min(h, maxY + padY);

        hotspotEl.style.left = `${(left / w) * 100}%`;
        hotspotEl.style.top = `${(top / h) * 100}%`;
        hotspotEl.style.width = `${((right - left) / w) * 100}%`;
        hotspotEl.style.height = `${((bottom - top) / h) * 100}%`;
      } catch {
        // Cross-origin (or other) canvas read failure — leave the
        // hotspot at its CSS default (the full layer box) rather than
        // breaking the hover entirely.
      }
    };

    if (sourceImg.complete) {
      run();
    } else {
      sourceImg.addEventListener("load", run, { once: true });
    }
  }

  // Palm tree hover swap (see the v8 comment at the top of the file).
  // No-ops entirely if `palmTreeImage` wasn't passed (no
  // `[data-palm-base]` element on the page); the hover/reveal itself
  // is plain CSS (`:hover` + `clip-path` transition, see the
  // stylesheet), so there's nothing else to wire up here even when
  // `palmTree80sImage` IS present.
  const palmBase = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>(
    "[data-palm-base]"
  );
  const palmHotspot = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
    "[data-palm-hotspot]"
  );

  if (palmBase && palmHotspot) {
    fitHotspotToAlpha(palmBase, palmHotspot);
  }

  // Back island frost hover reveal (see the v12 comment at the top of
  // the file). Same mechanism as the palm tree above, just scanning
  // `backIslandFrozenImage` itself instead of a separate "base" layer,
  // since the frost art IS the thing you're hovering to reveal — the
  // hotspot ends up sized to wherever the frost actually paints, not
  // the whole back island. No-ops if `backIslandFrozenImage` wasn't
  // passed (no `[data-frozen-overlay]` element on the page); like the
  // palm tree, the reveal itself is plain CSS now, nothing further to
  // wire up here.
  const frozenOverlay = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>(
    "[data-frozen-overlay]"
  );
  const frostHotspot = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(
    "[data-frost-hotspot]"
  );

  if (frozenOverlay && frostHotspot) {
    fitHotspotToAlpha(frozenOverlay, frostHotspot);
  }

  // Monado sword symbol cycling (see the v14 comment at the top of
  // the file). All symbol images share the same painted position (an
  // `inset: 0` layer like every other artwork here — see v6), so this
  // is pure show/hide bookkeeping, no positioning math needed: exactly
  // one `.hobby-monado-symbol` carries `.is-active` at a time, which
  // is what triggers its CSS flicker-in animation (see the
  // stylesheet). No-ops if `monadoImage`/`monadoSymbolImages` weren't
  // passed (no `[data-monado-symbol]` elements on the page).
  const monadoSymbols = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(
    "[data-monado-symbol]"
  );

  if (monadoSymbols.length) {
    const symbols = Array.from(monadoSymbols);
    let currentIndex = -1;

    const pickNextIndex = () => {
      if (symbols.length === 1) return 0;
      let next = currentIndex;
      while (next === currentIndex) {
        next = Math.floor(Math.random() * symbols.length);
      }
      return next;
    };

    const showSymbol = (index: number) => {
      if (currentIndex >= 0) {
        symbols[currentIndex].classList.remove("is-active");
      }
      currentIndex = index;
      symbols[currentIndex].classList.add("is-active");
    };

    // Show one immediately (rather than waiting for the first random
    // delay) so the sword's gem is never empty on page load — reduced
    // motion also relies on this: it never reschedules below, so this
    // first call is the ONLY thing that ever sets a symbol for those
    // visitors, and `.is-active`'s animation is disabled entirely in
    // that case (see the stylesheet), so it just appears, no flicker.
    showSymbol(pickNextIndex());

    if (!prefersReducedMotion) {
      const minDelay = 3000; // ms, shortest gap between symbol changes
      const maxDelay = 7000; // ms, longest gap between symbol changes

      const scheduleNext = () => {
        const delay = minDelay + Math.random() * (maxDelay - minDelay);
        setTimeout(() => {
          showSymbol(pickNextIndex());
          scheduleNext();
        }, delay);
      };

      scheduleNext();
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .hobby-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `overflow: hidden` here — see the comment on `overflow-x:
       hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal
       clipping page-wide, so the island group is free to sit taller
       than this section's own box without being hard-clipped. */
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: top;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    /* Seam-blend targets (see the shared comment in Layout.astro).
       Both edges target `--dusk-blue` — see the top-of-file comment
       above for why that's correct even though this section sits
       between two different neighbors: the actual background is a
       flat fill equal to that color, so it already matches
       AchievementIslandSection's bottom on this section's top edge,
       and already matches BonusIslandSection's top on this section's
       bottom edge, with no new variable needed. */
    --seam-target-top: var(--dusk-blue);
    --seam-target-bottom: var(--dusk-blue);
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
  }

  .body-text {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #444;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The whole island — one graphic made of 3 stacked layers, sized
     and positioned as a single unit. Normal flow (not absolutely
     positioned — see the v5 note at the top of the file), centered
     horizontally via the section's own `align-items: center`, with a
     little breathing room above it separating it from `.content`.
     `clamp()` keeps it readable on narrow screens without letting it
     dwarf the viewport on very wide ones — there's no viewport-HEIGHT
     term anywhere in this rule anymore, on purpose: normal flow means
     there's nothing below `.content` for the island to collide with,
     so nothing here needs to reason about how tall the viewport is. */
  .hobby-island-group {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: clamp(700px, 82vw, 1600px);
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: clamp(1.5rem, 5vh, 4rem) auto 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Fixes the group's proportions to your CSP canvas size (passed in
     via the `canvasWidth`/`canvasHeight` props, inline `style` above —
     see the v6 top-of-file comment) so every layer slot below always
     has a well-defined box to fill with `inset: 0`, regardless of how
     `width` above resolves at a given viewport size. All three
     `.hobby-layer-slot`s share this exact box at rest — it's only the
     drift/bob transforms further in that ever move a layer away from
     it. The `aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2160` here is just a fallback for
     the (non-Astro) preview harness / in case the inline style is ever
     stripped — the props above are the real source of truth. */
  .hobby-island-group-inner {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 3840 / 2160;
  }

  /* One slot per layer, all stacked on the exact same box via
     `inset: 0`. Stacking order is plain DOM order (back island first,
     Joy-Con second, front island last), so no explicit `z-index` is
     needed between them. */
  .hobby-layer-slot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* Bob loop lives on ITS OWN wrapper, one level up from the
     `.parallax-inner` element inside it — NOT on the same element as
     the mouse-repel drift. Both a CSS animation and the JS drift
     script ultimately set `transform` on whatever element they're
     attached to, and a running CSS animation wins that fight every
     frame (animations take precedence over inline styles for the
     animated property), which would silently make the JS-driven
     translate invisible even though the script keeps computing and
     setting it. Splitting them onto two nested elements — this one
     bobs via keyframes, the child below drifts via JS — is exactly
     how AchievementIslandSection avoids the same collision (its bob
     is on `.achievement-island-left`/`-right`, the drift is on the
     nested `.achievement-island-inner`). Each layer gets its own
     duration + a negative `animation-delay` (starts the loop already
     partway through) so the three don't bob in lockstep. */
  .hobby-layer-bob-back {
    animation: hobby-bob 6.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .hobby-layer-bob-joycon {
    animation: hobby-bob-joycon 5s ease-in-out -1.5s infinite;
  }

  .hobby-layer-bob-front {
    animation: hobby-bob 7.2s ease-in-out -3s infinite;
  }

  /* `.hobby-layer-bob-*` and `.hobby-layer-drift` are plain block
     divs with no explicit height — that's deliberate. Block elements
     already fill their parent's width by default, and each image's
     own intrinsic aspect ratio (all three source files share the same
     untrimmed CSP canvas size — see the v6 comment on `canvasWidth`/
     `canvasHeight` at the top of the file — matching
     `.hobby-island-group-inner`'s `aspect-ratio` above) takes care of
     height on its own once width is set — explicit height would just
     be indeterminate/ignored here anyway, since none of these three
     elements have a definite height to resolve a percentage against.
     `.hobby-layer-drift` is the mouse-repel target — see the
     `data-depth` attribute in the markup and the script above for how
     the three layers get different push strengths instead of moving
     as one unit. */
  .hobby-layer-bob-back,
  .hobby-layer-bob-joycon,
  .hobby-layer-bob-front,
  .hobby-layer-drift {
    width: 100%;
  }

  .hobby-layer-drift {
    position: relative;
  }

  .hobby-layer {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
  }

  /* v12 — was a randomly-timed `.is-active` animation (see the removed
     v7 mechanism); now a hover reveal instead, same pattern as the
     palm tree's `.hobby-palm-hotspot` (v8): `.hobby-frost-hotspot` is
     an invisible `pointer-events: auto` div, alpha-scanned by the
     script above to the frost art's own painted pixels (not the whole
     back island), and `.hobby-frozen-overlay` transitions its
     `clip-path` on hover instead of running a fixed-length keyframe
     loop — grows while the cursor is over the hotspot, withdraws the
     moment it leaves, for as long or short as the hover lasts, rather
     than a fixed 6.5s cycle that used to run on its own schedule.
     Sits on top of `backIslandImage` inside the SAME
     `.hobby-layer-drift` (see the v7 comment) via `position: absolute`
     — the sibling `backIslandImage` stays in normal flow and is what
     actually gives `.hobby-layer-drift` its height, so this overlay's
     `inset: 0` box always matches it exactly. */
  .hobby-frozen-overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    clip-path: circle(0% at 50% 50%);
    /* v13: slowed from 0.6s (the palm tree's speed, which this
       borrowed at first) to 1.1s, per your feedback — reads more like
       frost actually spreading/withdrawing rather than a quick UI
       swap. Deliberately NOT touching `.hobby-palm-base`/
       `.hobby-palm-80s`'s 0.6s — you didn't ask to slow those down,
       and they're a different kind of effect (a tree swap, not a
       spreading texture) where the quicker pace still fits. */
    transition: clip-path 1.1s ease;
  }

  /* Same reasoning as `.hobby-palm-hotspot` (v8): opts back into
     `pointer-events: auto` despite `.hobby-island-group`'s blanket
     `pointer-events: none`, which is what makes it hoverable at all.
     `inset: 0` is only the fallback until the script above sizes it to
     the frost art's real bounding box. Needs to come BEFORE
     `.hobby-frozen-overlay` in the markup for `~` below to reach it. */
  .hobby-frost-hotspot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  /* 150% deliberately overshoots — for a `circle()` radius without a
     `farthest-corner` keyword, 100% doesn't reliably reach the corners
     of a non-square box, and overshooting costs nothing here since a
     circle bigger than the image just means "fully visible," same as
     any other value past full coverage. */
  .hobby-frost-hotspot:hover ~ .hobby-frozen-overlay {
    clip-path: circle(150% at 50% 50%);
  }

  /* Palm tree hover swap (see the v8/v9 comments at the top of the
     file). `.hobby-palm-base` (`palmTreeImage`) and `.hobby-palm-80s`
     (`palmTree80sImage`) occupy the exact same box and use MATCHING
     `inset()` clip-paths run over the same duration/easing, moving in
     the same direction (top → bottom) — that's deliberate, not just
     two independent wipes. At any instant during the transition, the
     region `.hobby-palm-80s` has revealed so far is EXACTLY the region
     `.hobby-palm-base` has clipped away so far, so exactly one of the
     two is ever visible at a given point on the tree — no gap, and
     critically (per your feedback) no double-exposure where bits of
     the normal tree's silhouette peek out from behind the
     differently-shaped 80s art. It reads as a single scanning
     replacement sweeping down, not a cross-fade. */
  .hobby-palm-base,
  .hobby-palm-80s {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transition: clip-path 0.6s ease;
  }

  .hobby-palm-base {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
  }

  .hobby-palm-80s {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 100% 0);
  }

  /* Invisible, positioned at runtime by the script above (via inline
     `left`/`top`/`width`/`height`) to the real painted pixels of the
     palm tree — `inset: 0` here is only the fallback used until that
     script runs (or if it can't, e.g. a cross-origin image). Opts
     back into `pointer-events: auto` despite `.hobby-island-group`
     setting `pointer-events: none` on everything else in this
     section, which is what makes it hoverable at all. Also doubles as
     `.hobby-palm-scanline`'s positioning parent now (v10) — see below. */
  .hobby-palm-hotspot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  /* `~` reaches forward from the hotspot to these two — this only
     works because the hotspot comes FIRST in the markup (general
     sibling combinators can't look backwards; it's ahead of
     `.hobby-palm-base` specifically so it could reach that one too).
     Un-hovering just transitions everything back to its rest state
     above, so the whole swap reverses on its own with no extra rule
     needed. */
  .hobby-palm-hotspot:hover ~ .hobby-palm-base {
    clip-path: inset(100% 0 0 0);
  }

  /* v11 — layered a `filter` flicker on TOP of the clip-path
     transition (an `animation` running alongside it, not replacing
     it — they're independent properties, so this doesn't touch the
     v9 sync guarantee at all). Only on the way IN: the 80s art
     stutters through a couple of brightness/hue spikes as it locks
     in, like a signal settling, rather than smoothly brightening in —
     the normal tree still just clips cleanly away with no flicker of
     its own, so the "electronic" cue reads as the new image arriving
