     (2) thinned it from a soft 0.6%-tall gradient bar down to two
     crisp 1.5px hairlines. (3) "more electronic": split it into an
     offset cyan/magenta pair (RGB channels visibly mis-registered by a
     couple pixels — the CRT/VHS chromatic-aberration cue) and swapped
     the smooth `linear` sweep for `steps()`, so it jumps down in
     discrete increments like a hardware scanner or LED readout instead
     of gliding like a physical object. */
  .hobby-palm-scanline {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    height: 1.5px;
    background: #ff2fd6;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0.3em 0 rgba(255, 47, 214, 0.85);
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The cyan "ghost" channel, offset a couple pixels above the magenta
     line above — together they read as one RGB-fringed scan line
     rather than two, without needing a second real element in the
     markup. */
  .hobby-palm-scanline::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: -2px;
    height: 1.5px;
    background: #00f6ff;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0.3em 0 rgba(0, 246, 255, 0.85);
  }

  .hobby-palm-hotspot:hover .hobby-palm-scanline {
    animation: hobby-palm-scan 0.6s steps(14, end);
  }

